might have looked pretty boring to him, actually, compared to Burma and the East and sex and Orientalism and all that. I think he might have wanted to go. Do you think that there was perhaps a kind of strain of perversity that Eton fosters? And so if you become famous as a character at school and you're a character because you are perverse, because you do the things that are the opposite of what everyone else does, perhaps that sets you on a groove that you stick to for the rest of your life. And perhaps if everyone else is going to university, then you go off and join the police or something. It's a marker of how different you are. I think that's right. I mean, I didn't go to public school, but the nearest I came to it
